Mild Solar Erythema
Common Infections and Afflictions
Uniform erythema on exposed skin, Tender and hot sensation, Subsequent fine desquamation

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-031
Reason for Visit: Severe, burning pain across the shoulders and back following sun exposure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting acute, severe burning pain and generalized skin tightness following extensive ultraviolet exposure earlier in the day. The intensity of the pain is preventing sleep and causing significant localized distress. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a thorough dermatological examination, definitively rule out the development of widespread blistering (partial-thickness thermal injury) or symptoms of severe systemic sunstroke/heat exhaustion, and immediately institute aggressive topical and systemic analgesia to relieve the acute suffering. Delaying this assessment poses a risk of inadequate pain management and potential unrecognized systemic compromise, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My back is absolutely radiating heat, and the skin feels so tight it might snap if I move.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 5 hours ago following prolonged, unprotected ultraviolet exposure at the beach. The patient noted the gradual onset of uniform erythema strictly over sun-exposed skin (shoulders, upper back, posterior neck). The patient describes an intensely hot and tender sensation upon palpation, along with a distinct, severe tightness or drawing sensation of the skin. A clear demarcation line is visible between the exposed skin and areas that were covered by clothing. The patient completely denies any blistering, severe headache, confusion, nausea, systemic fevers, or rigors.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted by severe pain upon movement or clothing contact.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to the localized pain.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly uncomfortable, actively avoiding letting their back touch the chair.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Clinical diagnosis with no specific investigations typically required that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the upper back and shoulders reveals extensive, uniform, bright erythema. The affected skin demonstrates brisk and complete blanching upon light pressure. The skin is remarkably hot to the touch. The epidermal layer is entirely intact with a completely dry appearance and an absolute absence of blister formation. No edema is present. Sensation is fully intact to light touch. The patient is well-hydrated with moist mucous membranes. Neurological exam is normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a fan running directly on them.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has taken Paracetamol 1000mg with negligible relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mild Solar Erythema - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Partial-Thickness Sunburn - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of blister formation and the completely dry (rather than weeping) wound bed.
Polymorphous Light Eruption - Unlikely as the primary diagnosis given the classic delayed-onset (4-6 hours post-exposure) uniform confluent erythema, rather than discrete, highly pruritic papules or plaques developing days later.
Heat Exhaustion/Sunstroke - Ruled out clinically by the absence of confusion, severe headache, nausea, tachycardia, or severe dehydration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of extensive, painful thermal injury required immediate on-site clinical evaluation to accurately stage the depth of the burn and rule out systemic heat-related illness. Direct physical examination confirmed a superficial epidermal burn (first-degree solar erythema) without blistering or systemic compromise. By directly assessing the skin, initiating potent oral anti-inflammatory analgesia, and prescribing appropriate soothing topical therapy in the home, an unnecessary and highly distressing transfer to a hospital emergency department was successfully averted. This immediate clinical staging and pain management could not safely wait until standard consulting hours, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Advised regular Ibuprofen 400mg strictly with food up to three times daily for the next 48-72 hours to directly target the intense inflammatory cascade. Recommended Paracetamol 1000mg up to four times daily. Prescribed liberal application of a fragrance-free, aloe vera-based gel or bland emollient (e.g., Sorbolene) stored in the refrigerator, to be applied gently to the affected area four times daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: To instantly cool the skin and provide massive pain relief without further damaging the tissue, soak a clean cotton t-shirt in cool tap water, wring it out so it’s damp but not dripping, and wear it directly over the burn. The slow evaporation of the water physically pulls the trapped heat out of the deep layers of the skin, mimicking the body's natural sweating process but much more effectively.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days only if pain worsens significantly or if delayed blistering occurs.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the skin begins to form large fluid-filled blisters, if you develop a severe headache with confusion, or if you begin vomiting uncontrollably.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis reflects the current burn depth; solar injuries can occasionally declare their full depth (blistering) over 24 to 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The acute pain and heat will peak at around 24 hours post-exposure and gradually subside over the following days. Intense pruritus often develops as the skin begins to heal, followed inevitably by fine desquamation (peeling) after several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you stop passing urine entirely, or if you become extremely dizzy when standing up.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The UV rays damaged the very top layer of your skin, triggering a massive inflammatory response that dilates all the blood vessels (causing the redness and heat) and sensitizes the nerve endings (causing the extreme pain). The ibuprofen directly blocks the chemical that causes this inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Drink at least 3 liters of water over the next 24 hours; the burn draws fluid away from the rest of your body to the skin surface. Absolutely avoid any further sun exposure until the skin has completely finished peeling and returned to its normal color.

SMS INFORMATION
